WEBVTT

00:12:26.872 --> 00:12:30.385
you have two choices on how to step out
of your chief executive job."

00:12:30.409 --> 00:12:32.187
And I said, "Well, good, two choices.

00:12:32.211 --> 00:12:33.378
I like two choices.

00:12:33.402 --> 00:12:35.368
You know, it's like the Monty Hall game.

00:12:35.392 --> 00:12:37.606
You know, door number one
and door number two.

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:39.289
So what's behind door number one?"

00:12:39.313 --> 00:12:41.321
And he said, "Leave before you're ready."

00:12:41.345 --> 00:12:43.043
And I said, "Oh, I don't like that.

00:12:43.067 --> 00:12:44.591
What's behind door number two?"

00:12:44.615 --> 00:12:46.749
And he says, "Leave
on someone else's terms."

00:12:46.773 --> 00:12:48.694
I said, "Ooh, I really hate that one."

00:12:48.718 --> 00:12:50.583
AG: What did Arthur decide to do then?

00:12:50.607 --> 00:12:52.392
Exactly what he's suggesting you do.

00:12:52.416 --> 00:12:53.606
He went into teaching.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:55.964
AB: The bottom line is,
when the stakes are high,

00:12:55.988 --> 00:12:58.791
it's worth leaving things
before you're ready.

00:12:58.815 --> 00:13:02.011
AG: Arthur is content
with how his career choices worked out.